meta {
  name: Get health
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/health
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.status: eq ok
  res.body.name: eq api
  res.headers['x-minerva-request-id']: isDefined
}

docs {
  The liveness verdict, from `HealthUseCase` via `HealthController.root()`:

  ```json
  { "status": "ok", "name": "api" }
  ```

  Verdict only — it probes no database, queue or downstream service, so `ok` means this
  process is up and routing works and says nothing about anything behind it. `status` is
  the `ApiStatus` enum (`ok` | `error` | `pending`), which is why the assertion pins the
  value rather than only checking the field is there.

  `name` is what distinguishes this from the queue-coordinator's identical route, so a
  `baseUrl` pointing at the wrong service fails here instead of passing silently.

  `GET /health/live` and `GET /health/ready` answer with exactly this body — they exist so
  a Kubernetes manifest can bind each probe to its own path.

  The `X-MINERVA-REQUEST-ID` asserted above is on every response the api gives, this one
  included: `RequestIdMiddleware` keeps the id you sent if it is a uuid v4 and mints one
  otherwise. It is the id to quote when reporting what a route answered — `GET
  /health/status` is the only one that also puts it in the body. Sending your own is a
  collection-level header, disabled by default, because every route here accepts it.
}
